1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:04,002
Imagine a call arriving at Concrete's Customer Service Center.

2
00:00:04,002 --> 00:00:06,366
The agent answers through Genesys.

3
00:00:06,366 --> 00:00:10,476
but the caller is following up on an exchange request made last week.

4
00:00:10,476 --> 00:00:13,305
The information exists in Zoho Desk.

5
00:00:13,305 --> 00:00:16,220
The challenge is getting it to the right employee.

6
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:17,730
at the right moment.

7
00:00:17,730 --> 00:00:23,607
Today, as described in Concrete's current workflow, the agent may have to
copy the phone number,

8
00:00:23,607 --> 00:00:25,804
Search Zoho Desk manually.

9
00:00:25,804 --> 00:00:30,842
Open the customer's previous tickets and then look for the most recent
update.

10
00:00:30,842 --> 00:00:32,465
The search is possible.

11
00:00:32,465 --> 00:00:35,938
but every extra step interrupts the conversation.

12
00:00:35,938 --> 00:00:39,154
Now imagine a more connected experience.

13
00:00:39,154 --> 00:00:41,710
When an inbound call arrives in Genesys,

14
00:00:41,710 --> 00:00:44,953
the caller number starts an authorized customer lookup.

15
00:00:44,953 --> 00:00:47,203
if it finds one reliable match,

16
00:00:47,203 --> 00:00:50,534
A screen pop opens the relevant customer context.

17
00:00:50,534 --> 00:00:51,915
the contact record,

18
00:00:51,915 --> 00:00:53,514
Previous Support Tickets

19
00:00:53,514 --> 00:00:54,987
their current statuses,

20
00:00:54,987 --> 00:00:57,033
and the latest recorded update.

21
00:00:57,033 --> 00:00:59,511
Let's follow a realistic example.

22
00:00:59,511 --> 00:01:02,195
The customer is asking about an exchange.

23
00:01:02,195 --> 00:01:07,916
The agent can see an earlier ticket and a note saying the request is still
pending branch review.

24
00:01:07,916 --> 00:01:09,733
That's a recorded status.

25
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:12,226
not a promise that the review has happened.

26
00:01:12,226 --> 00:01:15,686
Instead of making the customer explain everything again,

27
00:01:15,686 --> 00:01:19,126
The agent can continue from the last documented point.

28
00:01:19,126 --> 00:01:21,347
If several contacts share a number,

29
00:01:21,347 --> 00:01:23,825
the employee chooses the correct record.

30
00:01:23,825 --> 00:01:25,167
If there's no match,

31
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:27,702
a safe manual search remains available.

32
00:01:27,702 --> 00:01:29,679
and if the lookup service fails,

33
00:01:29,679 --> 00:01:31,852
The call itself must continue.

34
00:01:31,852 --> 00:01:35,192
A phone number alone never proves someone's identity.

35
00:01:35,192 --> 00:01:37,232
Notice what's changing here.

36
00:01:37,232 --> 00:01:40,247
We're not replacing Genesys or Zoho Desk.

37
00:01:40,247 --> 00:01:43,651
and we're not pretending a new integration is already live.

38
00:01:43,651 --> 00:01:48,766
we're proposing a clearer way to connect systems that Concrete already
uses.

39
00:01:48,766 --> 00:01:51,218
The potential benefit is straightforward.

40
00:01:51,218 --> 00:01:52,975
fewer manual searches,

41
00:01:52,975 --> 00:01:54,254
easier follow-up.

42
00:01:54,254 --> 00:01:56,400
and more attention on the customer.

43
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,552
Those benefits must be measured in a real pilot.

44
00:01:59,552 --> 00:02:02,001
not presented as guaranteed savings.

45
00:02:02,001 --> 00:02:06,625
First, we'll review Concrete's existing Genesys pop-up configuration.

46
00:02:06,625 --> 00:02:08,357
Zoho Desk Access,

47
00:02:08,357 --> 00:02:09,333
permissions

48
00:02:09,333 --> 00:02:11,662
and the supported integration options.

49
00:02:11,662 --> 00:02:16,660
Then, we'll test the experience with authorized sample calls and validate
the results.

50
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:18,292
The goal is simple.

51
00:02:18,292 --> 00:02:20,155
when the conversation begins,

52
00:02:20,155 --> 00:02:22,071
the context should be ready.

53
00:02:22,071 --> 00:02:28,000
This is the Genesys and Zoho Desk experience proposed by GRINDCTRL for
Concrete.
